---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import AppearanceCompare from "../../components/Blog/AppearanceCompare.astro"
import Basics from "../../component-examples/switch/Basics.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="switch-checkbox">
  <section id="the-problem">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A switch is a checkbox that reads as on and off. Libraries tend to build
      it from a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> with <code>aria-checked</code>
      toggled in JavaScript, or a hidden checkbox plus extra spans for the track
      and the dot.
    </p>
    <p>
      The button throws away what the checkbox already does: toggling, Space to
      press, <code>:checked</code>, a form value and validation. The hidden
      checkbox keeps all that, but needs the extra spans to draw anything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-trick">
    <h2>The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>&lt;input type="checkbox" role="switch"&gt;</code> keeps the
        native behavior, the role makes assistive technology announce on and off
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>appearance: none</code> frees the input to draw its own
        pseudo-elements: {" "}<code>::before</code> is the track,
        <code>::after</code> is the dot
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:checked</code> moves the dot with <code>inset-inline-start</code
        >: track width minus dot minus gap, mirrored in right-to-left
      </li>
      <li>
        An <code>outline</code> in the dot's own color grows it without touching
        its box. <code>:active</code> grows it a little more
      </li>
      <li><code>light-dark()</code> picks the colors per color scheme</li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`[role="switch"] {
  --thumb-inset: calc((var(--track-block-size) - var(--thumb-size)) / 2);

  appearance: none;
  position: relative;
}

[role="switch"]::after {
  background-color: var(--thumb-bg-color);
  border-radius: var(--ui-radius-round);
  content: "";
  inset-inline-start: var(--thumb-inset);
  outline: var(--thumb-outline-width) solid var(--thumb-bg-color);
  outline-offset: -1px;
  position: absolute;
}

[role="switch"]:checked::after {
  --thumb-outline-width: calc(var(--thumb-inset) - 1px);

  inset-inline-start: calc(var(--track-inline-size) - var(--thumb-size) - var(--thumb-inset));
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="try-it">
    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      The same checkbox with and without <code>appearance: none</code>. Drag the
      size, and flip one: its twin follows.
    </p>
    <Example wide showCode={false}>
      <AppearanceCompare kind="switch" />
    </Example>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The <code>auto</code> column resets the library's rules to the browser's
        and keeps only the size, so it is a checkbox, whatever the role says
      </li>
      <li>
        With <code>none</code> the track follows
        <code>--ui-switch-track-block-size</code> and
        <code>--ui-switch-track-inline-size</code>, the dot follows
        <code>--ui-switch-thumb-size</code> (the demo sets all three from its
        slider), and the input keeps its checked state and keyboard handling
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each switch is a checkbox with <code>role=&quot;switch&quot;</code>.</p>
    <Example row showCode={false}>
      <Basics />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section id="fallback">
    <h2>Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        There is no <code>@supports</code>: <code>appearance: none</code>,
        pseudo-elements on a checkbox and logical insets work in every current
        engine
      </li>
      <li>
        The colors come from <code>light-dark()</code>. A browser without it
        can't resolve those custom properties, and the track and dot lose their
        colors
      </li>
      <li>
        Nothing transitions, in any motion setting: the dot moves the moment you
        click or tap, like Checkbox and Radio, so a press never looks missed
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
